eir hands on you.” Her new novel, “Future Home of the Living God,” hit the best-seller list last week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EXPECTING: Louise Erdrich’s new novel, “Future Home of the Living God,” drops off the hardcover fiction list after debuting last week at No. 13. As a work of straight-up science fiction, imagining evolution out of whack and the climate in (more) acute crisis, the book represents something of a departure for Erdrich. But she’s cooking with familiar ingredients: Ojibwe identity, the Minnesota landscape and — especially — sexual politics, as the government asserts a claim on pregnant women’s bodies. “I wanted to explore what a public creature you become when you’re pregnant, how everybody puts their hands on you,” Erdrich told Bethanne Patrick in The Los Angeles Times last month. “You’re vulnerable to an extreme degree when you’re pregnant.” To that end, the novel grapples openly with questions of choice: not necessarily abortion, since Erdrich’s pregnant heroine wants her baby, but with women’s freedom to control their biological destiny. “It’s so obvious to me that anti-choice rhetoric isn’t about religious faith,” Erdrich told Patrick. “That’s just a way for people to frame what they’re doing in a way that is palatable for them. Anti-choice is about controlling women’s bodies period. It’s about seizing control of young women.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/10/books/review/10erdrich-image/10erdrich-image-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 264, 'width': 256, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/10/books/review/10erdrich-image/10erdrich-image-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 264, 'width': 256, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'su