ction_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebecca R. Ruiz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Ruiz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7bb76a39-fab4-5556-8ddf-1e0bf6d1b26a', 'word_count': 749, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7bb76a39-fab4-5556-8ddf-1e0bf6d1b26a'}, {'abstract': 'We all lose because of the biases of Wall Street and Silicon Valley.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/02/opinion/sunday/the-cost-of-devaluing-women.html', 'snippet': 'We all lose because of the biases of Wall Street and Silicon Valley.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My first job out of college in the late 1980s was at Salomon Brothers, a trading house of cigar-smoking, expletive-spewing strivers. One day, I leaned over a colleague’s desk to work on a spreadsheet, and heard loud laughter from behind me; one of the guys was pretending to perform a sex act on me. Almost every day, I found a Xerox copy of male genitalia on my desk.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/03/opinion/sunday/03krawcheck/03krawcheck-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 464, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/12/03/opinion/sunday/03krawcheck/03krawcheck-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 464}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/03/opinion/sunday/03krawcheck/03krawcheck-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 791, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/03/opinion/sunday/03krawcheck/03krawcheck-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1582, 'width': 204