ost fascinating, and even moving, about the experimental works Arnold Schoenberg wrote in his mid-30s is how you hear a pioneering composer treading an uncertain path. Born in 1874, Schoenberg eventually concluded that the late Germanic Romanticism of his youth, with its hold on tonal (that is, major- and minor-key) harmony, had become an exhausted language. In several crucial pieces, starting around 1908, he slowly tried to find a new, atonal language.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/05/arts/05upshaw/05upshaw-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/12/05/arts/05upshaw/05upshaw-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/05/arts/05upshaw/05upshaw-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/05/arts/05upshaw/05upshaw-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/05/arts/05upshaw/05upshaw-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/12/05/arts/05upshaw/05upshaw-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/05/arts/05upshaw/05upshaw-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Exhausted by Harmony, Schoenberg Found Atonality', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brentano String Quartet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Museum of Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Botstein, Leon', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Upshaw, Dawn', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schoenberg, Arnold', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Erwartung (Opera)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-12-04T23:50:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/35b48d79-90c8-546b-8448-1092f42d5be7', 'word_count': 707, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35b48d79-90c8-546b-8448-1092f42d5be7'}, {'abstract': 'Christopher A. Wray, the F.B.I. director, did not mention the president in defending the agency’s work force as “focused on our critical mission.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/04/us/politics/christopher-wray-fbi-agents.html', 'snippet': 'Christopher A. Wray, the F.B.I. director, did not mention the president in defending the agency’s work force as “focused on our critical mission.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Christopher A. Wray, the F.B.I. director, defended his work force in an email on Monday, a day after President Trump said on Twitter that the agency’s standing was the “worst in History” and its reputation was in “Tatters.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'sou