ank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Bureau of Investigation', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dowd, John M', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-12-04T23:55:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/2290cdd7-cf09-5070-b95a-4eab7f75cdb9', 'word_count': 938, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2290cdd7-cf09-5070-b95a-4eab7f75cdb9'}, {'abstract': 'They never cared about deficits, but will gladly use them as an excuse to take away aid for those who need it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/04/opinion/republican-tax-bill-benefits.html', 'snippet': 'They never cared about deficits, but will gladly use them as an excuse to take away aid for those who need it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Republicans don’t care about budget deficits, and never did. They only pretend to care about deficits when one of two things is true: a Democrat is in the White House, and deficit rhetoric can be used to block his agenda, or they see an opportunity to slash social programs that help needy Americans, and can invoke deficits as an excuse. All of this has been obvious for years to anyone paying attention.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/05/opinion/05KRUGMAN/05KRUGMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 392, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/12/05/opinion/05KRUGMAN/05KRUGMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 392}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/05/opinion/05KRUGMAN/05KRUGMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 668, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/05/opinion/05KRUGMAN/05KRUGMAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1336, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/05/opinion/05KRUGMAN/05KRUGMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/12/05/opinion/05KRUGMAN/05KRUGMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/05/opinion/05KRUGMAN/05KRUGMAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Republicans Are Coming for Your Benefits', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Republicans Are Coming for Your Benefits', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inheritance and Estate Taxes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-12-04T23:55:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec88bffa-0181-5c29-a47d-72f561464c35', 'word_count': 863, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec88bffa-0181-5c29-a47d-72f561464c35'}, {'abstract': 'It’s simply not in service providers’ interests to throttle access to what their customers want to see.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/04/opinion/net-neutrality-overblown-concerns.html', 'snippet': 'It’s simply not in service providers’ interests to throttle access to what their customers want to see.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TORONTO — The Federal Communications Commission is planning to jettison  its network neutrality rules, and many Americans are distraught. Such a move, the Electronic Frontier Foundation warned, “invites a future where only the largest internet, cable and telephone companies survive, while every start-up, small business and new innovator is crowded out — and the voices of nonprofits and ordinary individuals are suppressed.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/05/opinion/05engelhart/05engelhart-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/12/05/opinion/05engelhart/05engelhart-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/05/opinion/05engelhart/05engelhart-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/05/opinion/05engelhart/05engelhart-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1152, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/05/opinion/05engelhart/05engelhart-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/12/05/opinion/05engelhart/05engelhart-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/05/opinion/05engelhart/05engelhart-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Concerns About Net Neutrality Are Overblown', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Losing Net Neutrality:  Nothing to Be Afraid Of', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Net Neutrality', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Communications Commission', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Antitrust Laws and Competition Issues', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-12-04T23:59:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Engelhart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Engelhart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/0eebcbb7-9999-5717-890f-fba2465aad5b', 'word_count': 856, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0eebcbb7-9999-5717-890f-fba2465aad5b'}, {'abstract': 'A follow-up fruitcake mix from King Arthur Flour adds candied citrus peels.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/04/dining/king-arthur-flour-yuletide-cheer-fruit-blend.html', 'snippet': 'A follow-up fruitcake mix from King Arthur Flour adds candied citrus peels.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As hands-on as you may be in the kitchen, making your own candied fruit might be taking ambition too far. I’ve done it and it’s a complex, time-consuming task that calls for boiling fruit or fruit peels in sugar syrup over and over again until the sugar replaces the water in the fruit. At King Arthur Flour in Vermont, they’ve assembled a nice shortcut with a package of evenly diced orange and lemon peels, golden raisins and cranberries. It’s a follow-up to their fruitcake mix of currants, raisins, dates, pineapple and cranberries, but it’s better with the citrus peels: King Arthur Flour Yuletide Cheer Fruit Blend, eight ounces, $12.95, kingarthurflour.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/06/dining/06burnerfruit-sub/merlin_130632239_f1e84c90-b011-4924-bb02-9f1808b46dd2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 472, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/12/06/dining/06burnerfruit-sub/merlin_130632239_f1e84c90-b011-4924-bb02-9f1808b46dd2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 472}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/06/dining/06burnerfruit-sub/merlin_130632239_f1e84c90-b011-4924-bb02-9f1808b46dd2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 805, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/06/dining/06burnerfruit-sub/merlin_130632239_f1e84c90-b011-4924-bb02-9f1808b46dd2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1610, 'width': 20