 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/25aa7ba0-156e-5f6d-a4fb-1e410fc3fe73', 'word_count': 1813, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25aa7ba0-156e-5f6d-a4fb-1e410fc3fe73'}, {'abstract': 'Children with special needs crave an opportunity to fit in and be accepted by playmates. That happens at Morgan’s Wonderland and its water park.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/05/opinion/morgans-wonderland-waterpark-kids-play.html', 'snippet': 'Children with special needs crave an opportunity to fit in and be accepted by playmates. That happens at Morgan’s Wonderland and its water park.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2006, while on a family vacation, Gordon Hartman, a San Antonio home builder, went to a hotel swimming pool with his daughter, Morgan. She was born with physical and cognitive disabilities; at 12, she had the cognitive age of a young child. Other children were swimming as well, two of them throwing a ball. As Hartman tells it, Morgan slowly made her way to them, and not being verbal, hit the ball. The frightened children collected their ball and scrambled out of the pool.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Place to Play, on Wheels or Feet', 'kicker': 'Fixes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disabilities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Morgan's Inspiration Island", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wheelchairs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AMUSEMENT AND THEME PARKS', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Centers for Disease Control and Prevention', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pittsburgh (Pa)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Antonio (Tex)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-12-05T10:45:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tina Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c32f94a-17c7-58e6-9557-9f486bb7c346', 'word_count': 1704, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c32f94a-17c7-58e6-9557-9f486bb7c346'}, {'abstract': 'More than any other politician, he led the push against Lyndon Johnson’s war — and broke his party in two.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/05/opinion/george-mcgovern-vietnam-democratic.html', 'snippet': 'More than any other politician, he led the push against Lyndon Johnson’s war — and broke his party in two.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Sept. 24, 1963, George McGovern, the junior senator from South Dakota, addressed a full chamber on America’s growing entanglement in Southeast Asia. His words rang like a fire bell in the night. “The current dilemma in Vietnam is a clear demonstration of the limitations of military power,” the 41-year-old Democrat declared, just before the vote on a record-breaking defense appropriation. “There in the jungles of Asia, our mighty nuclear arsenal, our $50 billion arms budget, and our costly ‘special forces’ have proved powerless to cope with a ragged band of illiterate guerrillas fighting with homemade weapons.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/05/opinion/-05Vietnam-Knock1/-05Vietnam-Knock1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 419, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/12/05/opinion/-05Vietnam-Knock1/-05Vietnam-Knock1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 419}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': No