1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8141b34f-72c8-5235-9174-a75ae1bd0a2f', 'word_count': 1606, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8141b34f-72c8-5235-9174-a75ae1bd0a2f'}, {'abstract': 'An app that completely alters your relationship to the outside world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/06/magazine/letter-of-recommendation-inaturalist.html', 'snippet': 'An app that completely alters your relationship to the outside world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One Christmas, when I was about 8, I received a modern bestiary, a large photo-dense encyclopedia of animals. I had loved animals from toddlerhood — and had begun assembling a small menagerie that would eventually include fish, a rabbit and a fire-bellied toad — but I was not yet acquainted with the staggering diversity of wildlife around the world. I would cradle that book on my lap for hours at a time, memorizing facts about the planet’s creatures, silently reciting their common and scientific names. Ever since, I have made it a point to learn the names of wildlife I encounter, seeking whatever tools might help me: hefty field guides to mushrooms, pocket-size pamphlets of backyard birds, archives of wildflower photos that let you search by petal color and bloom time.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/10/magazine/10mag-lor/10mag-10lor-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 437, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/12/10/magazine/10mag-lor/10mag-10lor-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 437}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/10/magazine/10mag-lor/10mag-10lor-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 746, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, '