me': 'subject', 'value': 'Collective Bargaining', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endowments', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graduate Schools and Students', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Harvard University', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Labor Relations Board', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-12-07T10:50:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vail Kohnert-Yount and Jared Odessky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vail', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kohnert-Yount', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jared', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Odessky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/4139b4a2-78b7-51d0-b0e8-a4fa6cf3dd24', 'word_count': 835, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4139b4a2-78b7-51d0-b0e8-a4fa6cf3dd24'}, {'abstract': 'Baby cereals made with oatmeal, barley, buckwheat, organic quinoa and wheat contained much lower amounts of inorganic arsenic than rice cereals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/07/well/eat/should-you-be-worried-about-the-arsenic-in-your-baby-food.html', 'snippet': 'Baby cereals made with oatmeal, barley, buckwheat, organic quinoa and wheat contained much lower amounts of inorganic arsenic than rice cereals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rice cereal is often a baby’s first solid food, but it contains relatively high amounts of arsenic, a source of growing concern. Now an advocacy group reports that while the levels of this potentially toxic substance in infant rice cereals have dropped slightly in recent years, rice cereals still contain six times more inorganic arsenic, on average, than infant cereals made with other grains like barley or oatmeal.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/12/well/baby-rice-photo/baby-rice-photo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 469, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/12/12/well/baby-rice-photo/baby-rice-photo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 469}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/12/well/baby-rice-photo/baby-rice-photo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/12/well/baby-rice-photo/baby-rice-photo-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1601, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/12/well/baby-rice-photo/baby-rice-photo-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/12/12/well/baby-rice-photo/baby-rice-photo-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/12/well/baby-rice-photo/baby-rice-photo-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Should You Be Worried About the Arsenic in Your Baby Food?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Too Much Arsenic in Baby’s Food?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baby Foods', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARSENIC', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cereals', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-12-07T11:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Well', 'subsection_name': 'Eat', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roni Caryn Rabin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roni', 'middlename': 'Caryn', 'lastname': 'Rabin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cdda4ee0-0e6d-5dda-8680-1b7580078925', 'word_count': 911, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cdda4ee0-0e6d-5dda-8680-1b7580078925'}, {'abstract': 'The Times’s critics give their choices of the best fiction and nonfiction works of the year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/07/books/critics-favorite-books-2017.html', 'snippet': 'The Times’s critics give their choices of the best fiction and nonfiction works of the year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This was a year when books — like the rest of us — tried to keep up with the news, and did a pretty good job of it. Novels about global interconnectedness, political violence and migration; deeply reported nonfiction accounts of racial and economic strife in the United States; stories both imagined and real about gender, desire and the role of beauty in the natural world. There were several worthy works of escapism, of course, but the literary world mostly reflected the gravity and tumult of the larger world. Below, The New York Times’s three daily book critics — Dwight Garner, Jennifer Senior and Parul Sehgal — share their thoughts about their favorites among the books they reviewed this year, each list alphabetical by author. Janet Maslin, a former staff critic who remains a frequent contributor to The Times, also lists her favorites.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/08/arts/08BOOKSOFTHEYEAR1/08BOOKSOFTHEYEAR1-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 356, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/08/arts/08BOOKSOFTHEYEAR1/08BOOKSOFTHEYEAR1-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1381, 'width': 480, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/08/arts/08BOOKSOFTHEYEAR1/08BOOKSOFTHEYEAR1-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/12/08/arts/08BOOKSOFTHEYEAR1/08BOOKSOFTHEYEAR1-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/08/arts/08BOOKSOFTHEYEAR1/08BOOKSOFTHEYEAR1-thumbLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Times Critics’ Top Books of 2017', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Times Critics’ Top Books of 2017', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Two Thousand Seventeen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-12-07T11:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dwight Garner, Jennifer Senior, Parul Sehgal and Janet Maslin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dwight', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Senior', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Parul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sehgal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}, {'firstname': 'Janet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maslin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 4}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/68a9b23e-74a3-5f39-b810-c36bce0d0088', 'word_count': 4524, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68a9b23e-74a3-5f39-b810-c36bce0d0088'}, {'abstract': 'As marijuana legalization and the opioid epidemic sweep across the country, new research is breathing fresh life into the perennial debate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/07/well/live/a-comeback-for-the-gateway-drug-theory.html', 'snippet': 'As marijuana legalization and the opioid epidemic sweep across the country, new research is breathing fresh life into the perennial debate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you grew up as part of the D.A.R.E. generation — kids of the 1980s and ’90s who learned about drugs from alarmist public service announcements — you know all too well the dangers of so-called gateway drugs. Go to bed with marijuana or beer, you were taught, and risk waking up with cocaine or heroin.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/11/29/well/well-bag/well-bag-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 899, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/11/29/well/well-bag/well-bag-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 899}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'im