https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/08/opinion/new-york-subway-closing.html', 'snippet': 'Shutting down the M.T.A. trains on weeknights could provide much-needed relief.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Politicians often shun the suggestion that they should avoid clichés like the plague, and officeholders in New York are no exception. Some of them have leaned extra hard lately on one of their more shopworn banalities: how theirs is the city that never sleeps.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/09/opinion/09sat2/09sat2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/12/09/opinion/09sat2/09sat2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/09/opinion/09sat2/09sat2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/09/opinion/09sat2/09sat2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/09/opinion/09sat2/09sat2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/12/09/opinion/09sat2/09sat2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/09/opinion/09sat2/09sat2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Let’s Talk About Closing the Subway', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Let’s Talk About Closing the Subway', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subways', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Transportation Authority', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Blasio, Bill', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Regional Plan Assn', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-12-09T01:35:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c963aa0-3514-5390-a571-7023f42c04c2', 'word_count': 556, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c963aa0-3514-5390-a571-7023f42c04c2'}, {'abstract': 'The idea that a candidate’s character doesn’t matter is pervasive and pernicious.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/08/opinion/roy-moore-scoundrel-theory-politics.html', 'snippet': 'The idea that a candidate’s character doesn’t matter is pervasive and pernicious.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THERE have always been and always will be scoundrels in political life, especially one particular genus — the hypocrite — whose private behavior diverges from his public actions. But there is also a novel and ominous concept of the statesman emerging in American politics, most clearly in the gymnastic contortions being accomplished to justify support for the Senate campaign of Roy Moore, the former chief justice of Alabama: the politician as a disembodied policy array.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/09/opinion/09