'nyt://article/4eee427b-979c-5311-857d-17d591e52cd2', 'word_count': 569, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4eee427b-979c-5311-857d-17d591e52cd2'}, {'abstract': 'Nixon said yes, and his decision has warped American foreign policy ever since.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/12/opinion/america-cambodia-bomb.html', 'snippet': 'Nixon said yes, and his decision has warped American foreign policy ever since.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is it acceptable to engage an enemy on the territory of a third country? It’s a question that has confronted Washington policymakers in recent years over ISIS forces in Syria and Qaeda operatives in Afghanistan and Pakistan. But in 1967, it was asked most urgently over the use of Cambodian territory by Vietnamese communists. What Washington policymakers decided then, and the way they continued to address the question over the following years, has had a significant impact on the shape of American foreign policy ever since.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/12/opinion/11VIETNAM-CUDDY1/11VIETNAM-CUDDY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 470, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/12/12/opinion/11VIETNAM-CUDDY1/11VIETNAM-CUDDY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 470}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/12/opinion/11VIETNAM-CUDDY1/11VIETNAM-CUDDY1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 801, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/12/opinion/11VIETNAM-CUDDY1/11VIETNAM-CUDDY1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1603, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image