Kaplan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rappeport', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/93c8d132-847e-5962-a8ff-9ed8d08513c8', 'word_count': 1458, 'uri': 'nyt://article/93c8d132-847e-5962-a8ff-9ed8d08513c8'}, {'abstract': 'Welcome to the Climate Fwd: newsletter. The New York Times climate team emails readers once a week with stories and insights about climate change. Sign up here to get it in your inbox.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/13/climate/climate-newsletter.html', 'snippet': 'Welcome to the Climate Fwd: newsletter. The New York Times climate team emails readers once a week with stories and insights about climate change. Sign up here to get it in your inbox.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Average air temperatures were so high last month at a monitoring station on the north coast of Alaska that computers rejected the readings as flawed. But there was nothing wrong with the data or the instrument that recorded it. Rather, temperatures had soared because of shrinking sea ice in the Arctic Ocean, one of the more obvious effects of climate change.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/11/climate/cli-newsletter-error/cli-newsletter-error-articleLarge.gif', 'height': 344, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/12/11/climate/cli-newsletter-error/cli-newsletter-error-articleLarge.gif', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 344}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/11/climate/cli-newsletter-error/cli-newsletter-error-jumbo.gif', 'height': 587, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumb