'2017-12-14T10:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Samin Nosrat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Samin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nosrat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0131186-b2cf-5573-82ff-a3205ccf5561', 'word_count': 640, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0131186-b2cf-5573-82ff-a3205ccf5561'}, {'abstract': 'A Vanderbilt neuroscientist has discovered an unusual but shockingly fruitful way to study our most enigmatic organ.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/14/magazine/to-unlock-the-brains-mysteries-puree-it.html', 'snippet': 'A Vanderbilt neuroscientist has discovered an unusual but shockingly fruitful way to study our most enigmatic organ.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One day in June 2012, at São Paulo’s international airport, Suzana Herculano-Houzel hauled two heavy suitcases onto an X-ray-machine conveyor belt. As the luggage passed through the scanner, the customs agent’s eyes widened. The suitcases did not contain clothes, toiletries or any of the usual accouterments of travel. Instead, they were stuffed with more than two dozen curiously wrapped bundles, each enclosing an amorphous blob suspended in liquid. The agent asked Herculano-Houzel to open her bags, suspecting that she was trying to smuggle fresh cheese into the country; two people had been caught doing exactly that just moments before.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/17/magazine/17mag-brainsoup1/17mag-17brainsoup-t_CA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 799, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/12/17/magazine/17mag-brainsoup1/17mag-17brainsoup-t_CA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlar