ne, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/17/magazine/17cover-type/17cover-type-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/12/17/magazine/17cover-type/17cover-type-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/17/magazine/17cover-type/17cover-type-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Behind the Cover: 12.17.17', 'kicker': 'Behind the Cover', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sexual Harassment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-12-15T10:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times Magazine', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df60b99a-0f20-586b-8898-4337ff644d80', 'word_count': 35, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df60b99a-0f20-586b-8898-4337ff644d80'}, {'abstract': 'In her best-selling essay collection, “The Last Black Unicorn,” the star of “Girls Trip” writes about growing up in South Central Los Angeles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/15/books/review/tiffany-haddish-last-black-unicorn-best-sellers.html', 'snippet': 'In her best-selling essay collection, “The Last Black Unicorn,” the star of “Girls Trip” writes about growing up in South Central Los Angeles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MAKING IT: Tiffany Haddish may have been spurned by the Golden Globes, but the comedian and “Girls Trip” star is holding her own on the hardcover nonfiction list — her book of personal essays, “The Last Black Unicorn,” debuts at No. 15. The book is, no surprise, every bit as rowdy as the movie. Haddish grew up in foster care in South Central Los Angeles, and for a time had a job as an “energy producer” at bar mitzvahs, where she was paid to dance enthusiastically and encourage other guests to have a good time. (“I was basically the Flava Flav of bar mitzvahs.”) In one memorable chapter, she explains how that gig led to a sideline pimping out her male friends. “If you’re doing bar mitzvahs, you’re going to meet old Jewish ladies,” she writes. “They started telling me how lonely they were and saying things like, ‘My husband’s not satisfying me.’” When one woman showed Haddish her collection of romance novels — “She had one that was like a slave thing or whatever. It was a big, strapping black guy on the cover, holding this passed-out white woman” — Haddish was inspired. “I don’t know any slaves,” she told the woman, “but I could probably hook you up with somebody big and strong.” Word spread, and Haddish thrived until the men realized they could cut their own deals without her. That’s O.K. She’s found other ways to put her people skills to lucrative use. On the copyright page, her publishers suggest stores file the book under “Biography and autobiography / Rich and famous.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/24/books/review/1224-BKS-LIST/1224-BKS-LIST-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 185, 'width': 185, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/24/books/review/1224-BKS-LIST/1224-BKS-LIST-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 185, 'width': 185, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/24/books/review/1224-BKS-LIST/1224-BKS-LIST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/12/24/books/review/1224-BKS-LIST/1224-BKS-LIST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/24/books/review/1224-BKS-LIST/1224-BKS-LIST-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tiffany Haddish on Bar Mitzvahs, Pimping and Other Rites of Passage', 'kicker': 'Inside the List', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Last Black Unicorn (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Haddish, Tiffany', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Darker: Fifty Shades Darker as Told by Christian (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'James, E L', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-12-15T10:00:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/112abdb1-f89f-5c90-8f4c-bbe7adad4c51', 'word_count': 449, 'uri': 'nyt://article/112abdb1-f89f-5c90-8f4c-bbe7adad4c51'}, {'abstract': '“I’m Just No Good at Rhyming,” the debut collection from Chris Harris and Lane Smith, includes the silly, the whimsical, the absurd and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/15/books/review/im-ju