': '2017-12-15T19:03:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bob Morris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eea4a779-3451-5273-9714-f25062eb934b', 'word_count': 1135, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eea4a779-3451-5273-9714-f25062eb934b'}, {'abstract': 'Change is glacial in Kota Yamazaki’s “Darkness Odyssey Part 2: I or Hallucination”; Indah Walsh’s “homespun” is playful and benign.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/15/arts/dance/how-to-fall-under-a-butoh-like-spell.html', 'snippet': 'Change is glacial in Kota Yamazaki’s “Darkness Odyssey Part 2: I or Hallucination”; Indah Walsh’s “homespun” is playful and benign.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The stage world of a Butoh performance is often a shock to the system for a Western audience. At first so little seems to be happening. When the performers move, they often seem stuck in ruts, teetering or twitching in states of insecurity, locked into tiny zones of space with small spasms and tics. Or they take ages to deliver arm or leg movements that most of us can do quickly and without effort. A few steps to and fro on half toe, the slow flexing of a foot, the vibration of a wrist, the roll of an isolated shoulder — these are often parts of the dance language here.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/15/arts/dance/merlin_131320690_5691a607-b659-4be2-bff7-b8e36148cc44/merlin_131320690_5691a607-b659-4be2-bff7-b8e36148cc44-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/12/15/arts/dance/merl