r': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Victoria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Namkung', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Eve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lyons', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f476583c-0aa7-53ca-9f1e-2aa65745eeb5', 'word_count': 1046, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f476583c-0aa7-53ca-9f1e-2aa65745eeb5'}, {'abstract': 'With sexual harassment revelations rife and public spaces threatened, some women are wondering just why they wear these things.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/16/fashion/the-end-of-high-heels.html', 'snippet': 'With sexual harassment revelations rife and public spaces threatened, some women are wondering just why they wear these things.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One Saturday evening this spring Florie Hutchinson, an arts publicist and a mother of three daughters, was nursing her youngest, Beatrice, at home and texting with a friend in Europe. She was exhausted after a day spent running errands in her hometown, Palo Alto, Calif., where she’d encountered back-to-back examples of everyday sexism: a children’s book that preached the importance of female politeness and a wall of bjorns and bassinets whose boxes uniformly depicted women caring for infants. So when she typed the word “shoe” into her phone and the red high-heel emoji appeared as a substitute, it felt like a sign.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/14/fashion/14HEELS-GIF/14HEELS-GIF-articleLarge.gif', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/12/14/fashion/14HEELS-GIF/14HEELS-GIF-articleLarge