role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/b4d24e0f-b707-5cab-8014-033f2e8f7396', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/b4d24e0f-b707-5cab-8014-033f2e8f7396'}, {'abstract': 'Before departing town and moving to Maine, the photographer Jessica Burstein donated many of her pictures to the New-York Historical Society.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/17/nyregion/grace-notes-jessica-burstein-leaving-new-york.html', 'snippet': 'Before departing town and moving to Maine, the photographer Jessica Burstein donated many of her pictures to the New-York Historical Society.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What Jessica Burstein had to show for just over 40 years in New York — photographs of what looked like crime scenes and of the new Yankee Stadium when it was under construction — had been sent across town for permanent safekeeping. The last two words in that sentence are not, in fact, redundant. The New-York Historical Society, where the photographs went, is not like the friend’s garage where you can stash a few things for a few months until you feel settled in your new place. Or the friend’s patience runs out.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/18/nyregion/18grace2/merlin_131172200_c84da235-4f87-49ea-a387-5a51c94e865d-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/12/18/nyregion/18grace2/merlin_131172200_c84da235-4f87-49ea-a387-5a51c94e865d-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/18/nyregion/18grace2/merlin_131172200_c84da235-4f87-49ea-a387-5a51c94e865d-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/18/nyregion/18grace2/merlin_131172200_c84da235-4f87-49ea-a387-5a51c94e865d-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/18/nyregion/18grace2/18grace1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/12/18/nyregion/18grace2/18grace1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/18/nyregion/18grace2/18grace1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why She’s Leaving Town: New York Just Isn’t Funny Anymore', 'kicker': 'Grace Notes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Packing Up a Camera That Captured Years of ‘Crime Scenes’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Law & Order (TV Program)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-12-17T15:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Barron', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barron', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0aa76660-392d-5940-9a38-128c9e550551', 'word_count': 1059, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0aa76660-392d-5940-9a38-128c9e550551'}, {'abstract': 'In Shafak’s new novel, “Three Daughters of Eve,” a woman attends a dinner party in Istanbul and flashes back to charged friendships during her college days.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/17/books/elif-shafak-three-daughters-of-eve-interview.html', 'snippet': 'In Shafak’s new novel, “Three Daughters of Eve,” a woman attends a dinner party in Istanbul and flashes back to charged friendships during her college days.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The three Muslim friends who meet at Oxford in the Turkish novelist Elif Shafak’s “Three Daughters of Eve” are described as the Sinner, the Believer and the Confused. “The question I wanted to explore was: Could women so different still be sisters?” Ms. Shafak said. “When women remain divided in patriarchal cultures, the only thing that benefits from this is patriarchy.” The novel is anchored in the present day, when Peri, 35, is attending an upper-crust dinner party in Istanbul. She remembers her time at Oxford in flashbacks throughout the night. The timely novel explores themes of feminism, religious devotion, secular doubt and political upheaval. Born in France, where her father was studying at the time, Ms. Shafak has lived in many places, including Spain and the United States. For the past eight years, she has divided her time between London and Istanbul. Below, she discusses the importance of women’s voices, the artists who helped her find her own confidence and more.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/18/books/18shafak-fivequestions1/18fivequestions1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 482, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/12/18/books/18shafak-fivequestions1/18fivequestions1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 482}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image'