ax Cuts Would Benefit Some Industries More Than Others', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corporate Taxes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goldman Sachs Group Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Pennsylvania', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Assn of Manufacturers', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wells Fargo & Company', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-12-18T23:51:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Washington', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Tankersley and Ben Casselman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tankersley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Casselman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6b317833-444d-5096-ac79-de24e3fa43f7', 'word_count': 857, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6b317833-444d-5096-ac79-de24e3fa43f7'}, {'abstract': 'With fragrant blood orange notes, Amaro dell’Etna is a festive holiday drink.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/18/dining/drinks/amaro-dell-etna.html', 'snippet': 'With fragrant blood orange notes, Amaro dell’Etna is a festive holiday drink.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Amaro, the traditional Italian herbal liqueur, is a perfect drink to follow holiday overindulgence. Amaro dell’Etna, which I first sampled at a cafe in Catania, the Sicilian city near where it has been made since 1901, is now available in the United States. It is distinguished by its herbal notes, hints of licorice and especially its fragrant essence of blood oranges, grown at the base of Mount Etna. And it is less sweet than some other popular brands of Italian amaro. Try it in a Boulevardier with bourbon and sweet vermouth: Amaro dell’Etna, $36.96, Astor Wines and Spirits, astorwines.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/20/dining/20burnerdrink/20burnerdrink-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 572, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/12/20/dining/20burnerdrink/20burnerdrink-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 572}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/20/dining/20burnerdrink/20burnerdrink-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 976, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/20/dining/20burnerdrink/20burnerdrink-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1953, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/20/dining/20burnerdrink/20burnerdrink-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/12/20/dining/20burnerdrink/20burnerdrink-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/20/dining/20burnerdrink/20burnerdrink-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Amaro From Mount Etna', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Sip: A Holiday Toast With Herbal Hints', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Liquor', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mount Etna (Italy)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-12-18T23:54:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'subsection_name': 'Wine, Beer & Cocktails', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b041a858-5e0b-51af-bcc9-528dd2160f22', 'word_count': 99, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b041a858-5e0b-51af-bcc9-528dd2160f22'}, {'abstract': 'Are Republicans more concerned with their bank accounts than with the country’s welfare?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/18/opinion/republicans-taxes-corruption.html', 'snippet': 'Are Republicans more concerned with their bank accounts than with the country’s welfare?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Unless something drastic happens, this will be the week Republicans ram through a tax cut that adds more than a trillion dollars to federal debt while undermining health care for millions. They will do so by violating all previous norms for major legislation, having held not a single hearing and rushed to a vote before the new senator from Alabama could be seated.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/19/opinion/19krugman/19krugman-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 817, 'width'