ument_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'subsection_name': 'Wine, Beer & Cocktails', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c91e4f8e-8539-53c8-a480-9bb1bf31ce8c', 'word_count': 112, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c91e4f8e-8539-53c8-a480-9bb1bf31ce8c'}, {'abstract': 'St. Johnsville Jr., a small soft-ripening goat cheese, makes a subtle statement on the cheese board.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/22/dining/goat-cheese-saxelby.html', 'snippet': 'St. Johnsville Jr., a small soft-ripening goat cheese, makes a subtle statement on the cheese board.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anne Saxelby considers St. Johnsville Jr. soft-ripening goat cheese as a paragon of subtle elegance. It has a somewhat rumpled, downy white rind. The six-ounce disk of cheese, about three inches across, has a chalky center that turns to satin when fully ripe. The aroma is herbal, and the flavor is mild and fresh with a hint of musk: St. Johnsville Jr. goat cheese, Cochran Farm 1790, St. Johnsville, N.Y.; $13 for six ounces at Saxelby Cheese in the Essex Street and Chelsea markets, saxelbycheese.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/27/dining/27burnercheese/27burnercheese-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 466, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/12/27/dining/27burnercheese/27burnercheese-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 466}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/27/dining/27burnercheese/27burnercheese-jumbo.jpg', 'height