essions of Nat Turner” was ensconced on top of the fiction list. But not without controversy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/22/books/review/william-styron-nat-turner-best-seller.html', 'snippet': 'Half a century ago, “The Confessions of Nat Turner” was ensconced on top of the fiction list. But not without controversy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HAPPY OLD YEAR: Half a century ago, in the winter after the Summer of Love, American reading habits were as turbulent as the culture at large. William Styron’s novel of slave rebellion, “The Confessions of Nat Turner,” ruled the fiction best-seller list of Dec. 31, 1967, where it was joined by titles as varied as “The Exhibitionist” (Henry Sutton’s sex-soaked Hollywood romp), “The Chosen” (Chaim Potok’s coming-of-age tale about Jewish identity) and “The President’s Plane Is Missing” (Robert J. Serling’s political thriller about a constitutional crisis). And — in maybe the clearest sign that you would have needed the Richter scale to measure the zeitgeist of the late 1960s — the same list made room for both “Christy,” Catherine Marshall’s historical Christian romance, and “Rosemary’s Baby,” Ira Levin’s tale of satanic horror. Strange days, as the Doors noted that fall.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/31/books/review/31styron/31styron-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 263, 'width': 256, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/31/books/review/31styron/31styron-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 263, 'width': 256, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/31/books/review/31styron/31styron-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75,