 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Megan Specia', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Megan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Specia', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d63ae9e4-0165-59a2-a9a1-a480e21e941d', 'word_count': 283, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d63ae9e4-0165-59a2-a9a1-a480e21e941d'}, {'abstract': 'The intelligence failure around the assault, more than the offensive itself, showed that American “victory” in Vietnam was impossible.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/23/opinion/vietnam-tet-offensive.html', 'snippet': 'The intelligence failure around the assault, more than the offensive itself, showed that American “victory” in Vietnam was impossible.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the Tet offensive ignited South Vietnam on Jan. 30, 1968, American forces were taken by surprise. Every one of the country’s 44 provinces was hit in a stunning, coordinated attack that changed the course of the war. With so many American resources in Vietnam focused on intelligence-gathering, why was the United States so clueless? My own experience with the United States Agency for International Development might shed some light on this lapse.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/27/opinion/23Vietnam-Oglesbyweb/merlin_131556539_124da4e5-dda7-4f66-ad2d-4f4207498567-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 405, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/12/27/opinion/23Vietnam-Oglesbyweb/merlin_131556539_124da4e5-dda7-4f66-ad2d-4f4207498567-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 405}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/27/opinion/23Vietnam-Oglesbyweb/merlin_1315565