apital Centre, or drumming in high school bands, Mr. Calilhanna felt part of a vital American culture.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/26/fashion/26DEADROCKERS-1/merlin_131644562_7232d13a-8d2c-42f6-b572-89d4ad7f170e-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 788, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/12/26/fashion/26DEADROCKERS-1/merlin_131644562_7232d13a-8d2c-42f6-b572-89d4ad7f170e-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 788}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/26/fashion/26DEADROCKERS-1/merlin_131644562_7232d13a-8d2c-42f6-b572-89d4ad7f170e-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 780, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/26/fashion/26DEADROCKERS-1/merlin_131644562_7232d13a-8d2c-42f6-b572-89d4ad7f170e-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1560, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/26/fashion/26DEADROCKERS-1/26DEADROCKERS-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/12/26/fashion/26DEADROCKERS-1/26DEADROCKERS-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/26/fashion/26DEADROCKERS-1/26DEADROCKERS-1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Avalanche of Rock ’n’ Roll Death', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pop and Rock Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Death and Dying', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-12-26T21:59:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Kurutz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kurutz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a64af880-6a7c-5ed5-81ef-8c5d6188d399', 'word_count': 1096, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a64af880-6a7c-5ed5-81ef-8c5d6188d399'}, {'abstract': 'The author of the On Comedy column shares his philosophy on printing punch lines, how he decides what to review and why he laughs less than he used to.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/26/insider/whats-so-funny-the-timess-comedy-critic-explains.html', 'snippet': 'The author of the On Comedy column shares his philosophy on printing punch lines, how he decides what to review and why he laughs less than he used to.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Are punch lines spoilers? Since a film review isn’t supposed to reveal the end of a movie, should a comedy review avoid divulging the big twist in a joke? As the comedy critic for The New York Times, I regularly struggle with this question.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/26/pageoneplus/26a2ittzinoman/26a2ittzinoman-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/12/26/pageoneplus/26a2ittzinoman/26a2ittzinoman-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {