18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Norman Mayersohn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Norman', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mayersohn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ae58f86-d9e8-5c88-b11b-7cd4b0b13eca', 'word_count': 1121, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ae58f86-d9e8-5c88-b11b-7cd4b0b13eca'}, {'abstract': 'President Trump’s broadside aimed at Vanity Fair mastered a tricky title and made much of a 14-word statement.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/28/business/media/trump-tweet-vanity-fair.html', 'snippet': 'President Trump’s broadside aimed at Vanity Fair mastered a tricky title and made much of a 14-word statement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Trump, vacationing in Palm Beach, Fla., was relatively subdued on Twitter this week — at least until Thursday, when he launched a somewhat arcane broadside at the magazine Vanity Fair.  The tweet, dense by Trumpian standards, was a bit confounding, given its highly specific references to rumors and tensions within the Manhattan magazine industry, and a minor Twitter kerfuffle involving a 63-second video about Hillary Clinton. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/28/business/media/trumpmediatweet2/trumpmediatweet2-articleLarge.png', 'height': 385, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/12/28/business/media/trumpmediatweet2/trumpmediatweet2-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 385}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/12/28/business/media/trumpmediatweet2/trumpmediatweet2-jumbo.png', 'height': 657, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumb