0', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Dwyer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dwyer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d38d2ce-ed8e-5d24-aab4-2f8496695e5f', 'word_count': 805, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d38d2ce-ed8e-5d24-aab4-2f8496695e5f'}, {'abstract': 'Yuzuri, a Japanese drink made from whole yuzu fruits steeped in a rice-based spirit, adds a different dimension to traditionally lemony cocktails.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/29/dining/drinks/yuzuri-yuzu-liqueur-adds-tart-citrus-flavors-to-cocktails.html', 'snippet': 'Yuzuri, a Japanese drink made from whole yuzu fruits steeped in a rice-based spirit, adds a different dimension to traditionally lemony cocktails.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Yuzu, the lemony citrus fruit that’s a staple in Japan, provides the essential flavor and aroma for Yuzuri, a new Japanese liqueur. The whole fruit — peels, juice and pulp — steeps for a month in a spirit made from rice. The liqueur is then lightly sweetened. The result is almost colorless, with a flavor that’s citrusy, hinting of lemongrass and more tart than sweet. I’d keep it on the bar to add a new dimension to tart-sweet cocktails like a sidecar or a whiskey sour: Yuzuri, $37.99 for 750 milliliters, Hi-Time Wine Cellars, Costa Mesa. Calif., hitimewine.net.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/03/dining/03BURNERLIQUEUR/03BURNERLIQUEUR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 805, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/03/dining/03BURNERLIQUEUR/03BURNERLIQUEUR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 805}}, {'rank': 0, 