95, a nation was rapt as three frogs croaked the syllables in “Budweiser.” Four years later, Budweiser prompted countless television viewers to wag their tongues and ask their friends, “Whassup?”', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/01/business/01XP-DILLY1/merlin_131701664_832fed20-3cec-4a2d-b8b3-164e2d776124-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 310, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/01/business/01XP-DILLY1/merlin_131701664_832fed20-3cec-4a2d-b8b3-164e2d776124-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 310}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/01/business/01XP-DILLY1/merlin_131701664_832fed20-3cec-4a2d-b8b3-164e2d776124-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 529, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/01/business/01XP-DILLY1/merlin_131701664_832fed20-3cec-4a2d-b8b3-164e2d776124-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1058, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/01/business/01XP-DILLY1/31XP-DILLY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/01/01/business/01XP-DILLY1/31XP-DILLY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/01/business/01XP-DILLY1/31XP-DILLY1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Do You Turn an Ad Into a Meme? Two Words: Dilly Dilly', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dilly Dilly, and the Alchemy of Meme-Making', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Anheuser-Busch InBev NV', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beer', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-12-31T20:54:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel Victor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Victor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0985719-04b7-5ade-95f0-94d3572319fe', 'word_count': 762, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0985719-04b7-5ade-95f0-94d3572319fe'}, {'abstract': 'Jacqueline Jones discusses her new biography of Lucy Parsons, a woman born into slavery who became an outspoken advocate for the working classes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/31/books/tell-us-5-things-about-your-book-goddess-of-anarchy.html', 'snippet': 'Jacqueline Jones discusses her new biography of Lucy Parsons, a woman born into slavery who became an outspoken advocate for the working classes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lucy Parsons would stand out today, so one can imagine the scene she created as a woman in the second half of the 19th century espousing anarchy for the purpose of overthrowing capitalism. An African-American born into slavery, Parsons grew up to fashion a story about herself, claiming to be the daughter of Mexican and Native American parents. In her new biography, “Goddess of Anarchy,” Jacqueline Jones writes that Parsons “rejected a personal historical or ethnic identity in favor of presenting herself as the champion of the laboring classes; that, she thought, was all that people needed to know about her.” Ms. Jones, who won the Bancroft Prize in 1986 for her book “Labor of Love, Labor of Sorrow: Black Women, Work, and the Family, from Slavery to the Present,” teaches in the history department at the University of Texas at Austin. Below, she talks about the new information she unearthed about Parsons, the way Parsons captivated crowds and much more.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/01/arts/01jones-fivequestions1/01jones-fivequestions1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 861, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/01/arts/01jones-fivequestions1/01jones-fivequestions1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 861}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/01/arts/01jones-fivequestions1/01jones-fivequestions1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 714, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/01/arts/01jones-fivequestions1/01jones-fivequestions1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1428, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/01/arts/01jones-fivequestions1/01jones-fivequestions1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/01/01/arts/01jones-fivequestions1/01jones-fivequestions1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/01/arts/01jones-fivequestions1/01jones-fivequestions1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tell Us 5 Things About Your Book: ‘Goddess of Anarchy’', 'kicker': 'Q. & A.', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dodging the Law and Championing Workers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jones, Jacqueline', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Goddess of Anarchy: The Life and Times of Lucy Parsons, American Radical (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Parsons, Lucy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-12-31T21:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/7065a19f-d546-509e-bdf7-e0ec039dfa01', 'word_count': 972, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7065a19f-d546-509e-bdf7-e0ec039dfa01'}, {'abstract': 'At a public forum with Times readers, survivors reconnect to discuss if and how they can “find the humanity.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/12/31/reader-center/how-a-reporter-and-victims-families-went-beyond-the-horror-of-2-kansas-hate-crimes.html', 'snippet': 'At a public forum with Times readers, survivors reconnect to discuss if and how they can “find the humanity.”', 'lead_par